  "result": "The premise isn't true: more agents don't always give better results. So \"five or ten?\" is the wrong question. The right one is whether you need more than one.\n\n- **Multi-agent system:** several model-driven loops, each with its own instructions and tools, passing work between them.\n- **Why it's tempting:** specialists sound better than one generalist.\n- **What it costs:**\n  - Every agent is its own loop of model calls, so the token bill grows with each one.\n  - Every handoff between agents can lose or garble information.\n  - More moving parts are harder to debug and harder to defend at a design review.\n- **Sports analogy:** a soccer team with 20 players on the pitch isn't better than 11. Extra players mean more passes, more miscommunication and more people to pay. Add a player only when a role needs one.\n- **When splitting pays off:**\n  - The tasks really differ, for example needing very different tools.\n  - One context window is too crowded.\n  - Pieces can run in parallel.\n- **For your assistant:** answering from manuals, checking the calendar and booking all fit one agent with three tools. Start there, and split only if you can name a specific failure it causes.\n\nQuick check: what would be the concrete cost of splitting \"check the calendar\" and \"book equipment\" into two agents?",
